---
import { TESTFLIGHT } from "../../../data/site.ts";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="faq"
  title="Questions and answers"
  description="Answers to common questions about Deptheos: license, hardware, RAW and linear DNG, privacy, EXIF metadata, and the iOS app."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Questions and answers</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Short answers to the questions people ask most, each with a link to the page that says more.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="before">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Before you install</h2>

      <h3 id="free">Is it really free? What is the license?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. Deptheos is free and open source under the GNU General Public License version 3, with added
        permissions to combine it with the Adobe DNG SDK and XMP Toolkit and to distribute it through app
        stores. The source and the full terms are on GitHub, in
        <a href="https://github.com/ryanmitts/deptheos/blob/main/LICENSE.md">LICENSE.md</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="systems">Which systems does it run on? Is there a version for Intel Macs?</h3>
      <p>
        macOS 27 or later on Apple silicon, Windows 10 or later (64-bit), and, as a preview, Linux on x86-64 or ARM64 with a
        Vulkan driver. There is no build for Intel Macs. <a href="/download/">Download</a> has the file
        for each system, and <a href="/docs/install/">Installing</a> the steps.
      </p>

      <h3 id="gpu">Do I need a graphics card?</h3>
      <p>
        Stacking runs on the GPU: Metal on Apple devices, DirectX 12 or Vulkan on Windows, Vulkan on Linux.
        Integrated graphics will do. A Windows or Linux computer with no GPU stacks on the CPU instead, much more
        slowly. Where a GPU is there but doesn't work, choose software rendering in Settings ▸ Advanced.
        <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#gpu">Troubleshooting</a> explains how.
      </p>

      <h3 id="memory">How much memory does it need?</h3>
      <p>
        16 GB is recommended. The memory a stack needs grows with the size of the photos, not with the number
        of frames. On a Mac, iPhone or iPad, Deptheos checks before it starts whether the stack fits in the memory
        the GPU can use, and says so when it won't; <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#gpu">Memory and the GPU</a>
        has what to try.
      </p>

      <h3 id="privacy">Does it send my photos anywhere?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Stacking happens on your device, and your frames, renders and logs stay there. The desktop app's
        one request over the network is the update check: at startup it asks GitHub (api.github.com) for
        Deptheos's latest release, sending only the app's version, and you can turn it off under Settings ▸
        General ▸ <em>Check for updates</em>. The command line makes no requests at all; the Help menu's pages
        open in your browser. <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> says exactly what is sent.
      </p>

      <h3 id="ios">Is there an iPhone or iPad app?</h3>
      <p>
        It is in public beta on <a href={TESTFLIGHT}>TestFlight</a>, and coming to the App Store, with the same engine, a camera for shooting brackets, and Apple
        Pencil for retouching on iPad. It needs iPhone 15 Pro or newer, or an iPad with an M-series or A17 Pro
        chip or newer, on iOS 26 or iPadOS 26; TestFlight and the App Store offer it only on those devices. <a href="/iphone/">Deptheos for iPhone and iPad</a> has the
        details.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="stacking">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Stacking</h2>

      <h3 id="frames">How many frames can it stack?</h3>
      <p>
        There is no set limit. Only a few frames are in memory at once, so memory use doesn't grow with the
        number of frames, only with the size of each photo; more frames take longer, and need nothing more. The
        home page's <a href="/#speed">stacking times</a> include stacks of 200 frames.
      </p>

      <h3 id="method">Which method should I use?</h3>
      <p>
        Start with Pyramid, the default, which keeps hairs and fine crossing detail crisp. Try Depth when a
        plain background comes out grainy or a dark area beside something bright looks hazy. When each does
        better in a different part of the picture, stack with both and paint from one onto the other.
        <a href="/docs/methods/">Choosing a method</a> compares all three, Wavelet included.
      </p>

      <h3 id="handheld">Do I need a tripod, or can I stack handheld?</h3>
      <p>
        Deptheos aligns the frames before stacking, so a handheld stack can work. The default aligner, ECC, handles
        a tripod, a focus rail and most handheld brackets; switch Alignment to <em>Grid search</em> when frames
        jump further than ECC can track. Alignment moves each frame as a whole, so a subject that moved, or
        parallax between near and far parts, has to be fixed by retouching. <a href="/docs/alignment/">Aligning
        the frames</a> explains both aligners.
      </p>

      <h3 id="sharpen">Does it sharpen the result?</h3>
      <p>
        Not on its own. No sharpening or contrast is added after stacking, so the detail in the result is the
        detail in your frames. To sharpen it, use Sharpening in <a href="/docs/adjust/">Adjust</a>, or your
        editor after exporting. <a href="/features/#stacking">Features</a> lists what each method does.
      </p>

      <h3 id="left-out">Why were some frames left out?</h3>
      <p>
        A frame that can't be read or lined up with the others is left out, and the stack goes on without it.
        The notice when the stack finishes names each one and why. When alignment is the reason, stack again
        with the grid search. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#frames">Troubleshooting</a> covers the messages.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="files">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Files and color</h2>

      <h3 id="raw">Which RAW files does it read? When do I need Adobe DNG Converter?</h3>
      <p>
        Camera RAW files that LibRaw reads, such as CR3, NEF, ARW, RAF, ORF, and RW2, and DNG, as well as JPEG,
        TIFF, and PNG. RAW and DNG frames are stacked as linear camera data. LibRaw is built in and works out of
        the box; install Adobe DNG Converter (macOS and Windows) only if you want Adobe's own demosaic or shoot
        with a brand-new camera not yet supported by LibRaw. <a href="/docs/raw/">RAW, DNG and color</a> has the
        full details.
      </p>

      <h3 id="flat-raw">Why do stacked RAW files look flatter than in Lightroom or my camera?</h3>
      <p>
        Deptheos stacks linear RAW data without applying auto-tone, sharpening, or a camera picture style. Set
        exposure, contrast, highlights, shadows and the rest in <a href="/docs/adjust/">Adjust</a>, or export
        as a 16-bit linear DNG and open it in Lightroom, Camera Raw, or your RAW editor to develop it just like
        an original RAW file.
      </p>

      <h3 id="exif">Does it preserve EXIF metadata?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. Exports keep the camera, lens, exposure, capture time, artist, and copyright from the reference
        frame, plus the names of the stacked files. GPS location, serial numbers, and maker notes are left out
        (<a href="/docs/export/#metadata">Metadata</a>).
      </p>

      <h3 id="dng">Can I get a DNG back? Why is DNG dimmed for my JPEG stack?</h3>
      <p>
        A stack of RAW or DNG frames can be exported as a 16-bit linear DNG, which Lightroom, Camera Raw and
        other RAW editors develop like a RAW file. A stack of JPEG, TIFF or PNG frames has no linear camera data
        to put in one, since those frames are finished pictures, so DNG is dimmed for it; export TIFF instead.
        <a href="/docs/export/#formats">Exporting</a> covers each format.
      </p>

      <h3 id="originals">Does it change my original files?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Your frames are only ever read: Deptheos never changes, moves or deletes them, and deleting a render
        or a stack leaves them alone. <a href="/docs/concepts/#files">Stacks, renders and the Library</a> says
        more.
      </p>

      <h3 id="renders">Where are my renders kept?</h3>
      <p>
        In the app's own folder, not beside your photos: <code>~/Library/Application Support/Deptheos</code> on
        macOS, <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Deptheos</code> on Windows and <code>~/.local/share/Deptheos</code> on Linux.
        Settings ▸ Library ▸ <em>Library folder</em> chooses another folder for the next time Deptheos starts
        (it does not move existing renders; copy them first if you want to keep them).
        <a href="/docs/install/#data">Where it keeps its data</a> lists the settings and logs folders too.
      </p>

      <h3 id="lightroom">Can I use it from Lightroom? Is there a plugin?</h3>
      <p>
        There is no plugin. Add the RAW files themselves to Deptheos, or export the frames from your editor as
        TIFFs and add those, then bring the result back: a DNG result from RAW frames opens in Lightroom as a
        RAW file, with the camera's color profile and white balance still adjustable.
        <a href="/docs/raw/#keeps">What a DNG result keeps</a> explains what carries over.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="problems">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Problems</h2>

      <h3 id="bug">How do I report a bug?</h3>
      <p>
        Choose Help ▸ <em>Report a Problem…</em>, which opens a new issue on GitHub, and say what you did and
        what happened. Issues are public, so the diagnostics go by email instead: Help ▸ <em>Save
        Diagnostics…</em> saves the app's logs as one zip file to send to
        <a href="mailto:support@deptheos.com">support@deptheos.com</a>. On Windows and Linux the Help menu is
        under the ≡ button. <a href="/support/">Support</a> has the details, for the iPhone and iPad app too.
      </p>

      <h3 id="speed">How fast is it on my computer?</h3>
      <p>
        Run <code>deptheos bench &lt;folder&gt; --live --repeat 5 --cool 30</code> on a folder of your own frames.
        It times whole stacks as the app runs them, with Pyramid and Depth and each aligner, and prints a table of
        the times. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#timings">Timings</a> explains the options, and
        <a href="/docs/cli/#install">The command line</a> says where the command is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
